WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.441
The Linux GPU stack includes several layers that have to line up.

00:00:04.441 --> 00:00:09.907
Kernel modules provide low-level driver functionality inside the running kernel.

00:00:09.907 --> 00:00:12.709
Firmware may be required by the hardware.

00:00:12.709 --> 00:00:18.380
User-space driver libraries provide interfaces that applications and runtimes call.

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:25.759
Display drivers may support monitors and desktops, while compute runtimes support GPU-accelerated workloads.

00:00:25.759 --> 00:00:33.480
Package managers install and update many of these pieces, but AI workloads often depend on more than one package.

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:37.170
Kernel headers may be needed to build a driver module.

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:42.089
DKMS, at a high level, helps rebuild kernel modules when kernels change.

00:00:42.089 --> 00:00:45.984
Device nodes expose GPU hardware to user-space processes.

00:00:45.984 --> 00:00:51.791
Permissions determine which users, services, and containers can access those devices.

00:00:51.791 --> 00:00:57.394
A working desktop display does not automatically mean compute workloads are ready.

00:00:57.394 --> 00:01:09.215
Likewise, a headless inference server may not need a full desktop stack, but it still needs the right kernel support, firmware, user-space libraries, and runtime interfaces.

00:01:09.215 --> 00:01:16.594
The exact package names vary by distribution and vendor, but the architectural responsibilities are similar.

00:01:16.594 --> 00:01:21.035
Understanding these layers makes troubleshooting less mysterious.

00:01:21.035 --> 00:01:26.023
If the GPU is not visible, look at hardware detection and kernel modules.

00:01:26.023 --> 00:01:33.129
If the driver is loaded but an AI library fails, look at user-space libraries and runtime compatibility.

00:01:33.129 --> 00:01:40.508
If containers cannot see the GPU, look at device exposure, container runtime configuration, and permissions.

00:01:40.508 --> 00:01:43.651
AI acceleration depends on the complete stack.
